msgid "update"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:67
msgid "`set` returns a new matrix"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:67
msgid "`set` writes in place"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:68
#: manual/design/immut.md:70
msgid "not available"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:68
#: manual/design/immut.md:70
msgid "available"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:69
msgid "determinant"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:69
msgid "Bareiss, exact over integral domains"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:69
msgid "LU with tolerance, floating point"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:70
msgid "decompositions, inverse, statistics"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:71
msgid "not on `Vector` (see `ImmutableDenseVector::dot`)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:73
msgid "No subtraction on `Vector`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:75
msgid "`Vector` implements `Add`, `Mul` and `Neg` but not `Sub`; `u - v` is written `u + -v`. This is an asymmetry inherited from earlier releases, not a mathematical statement. The `backends/default` wrappers provide `-`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:81
msgid "Value semantics and referential transparency"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:83
msgid "An expression is referentially transparent when it can be replaced by its value without changing the program. With immutable matrices,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:91
msgid "so any expression mentioning $A$ means the same thing on either side of the update. Algebraic identities can then be used directly as program transformations: $(A + B) + C$ and $A + (B + C)$ denote the same value for exact scalars, and an intermediate result may be reused or recomputed at will."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:96
msgid "Persistent storage"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:98
msgid "Entries live in row-major order in `moonbitlang/core/immut/vector`, a persistent vector implemented as a trie with branching factor 32. Replacing one element copies the path from the root to the leaf, one node of at most 32 slots per level, and shares every other node, so"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:108
msgid "and the old matrix stays valid. Reads also cost $O(\\log_{32} N)$; since $32^6 = 2^{30}$ and $32^7 = 2^{35}$, at most seven levels cover every size an `Int` index can reach. Whole-matrix operations rebuild the trie in $O(N)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:112
msgid "Matrix powers by repeated squaring"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:114
msgid "For a square matrix over a semiring, write $k = \\sum_t b_t 2^t$ in binary. Then"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:120
msgid "The rearrangement of the product is valid because matrix multiplication is associative over any semiring (see the [algebra design](algebra.md)); commutativity of the scalars is not needed, since all factors are powers of the same $A$ and powers of one element of an associative ring commute with each other. `pow` keeps a state $S$, an exponent $e$ and a base $B$ with the invariant"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:131
msgid "initially $(I, k, A)$. If $e$ is odd, $S B^{e} = (S B) B^{e-1}$ and $B^{e-1} = (B^2)^{\\lfloor e/2 \\rfloor}$; if $e$ is even, $B^{e} = (B^2)^{e/2}$. So the step $(S, e, B) \\mapsto (S B^{e \\bmod 2}, \\lfloor e/2 \\rfloor, B^2)$ preserves the invariant, and $e$ strictly decreases. The loop stops at $e = 1$ with $S \\cdot B$, or at $e = 0$ (only for $k = 0$) with $S = I$. It squares $\\lfloor \\log_2 k \\rfloor$ times and multiplies into $S$ once per 1-bit of $k$, so it performs"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:144
msgid "matrix products instead of $k - 1$; the first product into $S$ is by $I$. Over $n \\times n$ matrices that is $O(n^3 \\log k)$ scalar operations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:147
msgid "For fixed-width integers the result is exact in $\\mathbb{Z}/2^{w}\\mathbb{Z}$ ($w = 32$ for `Int`, $64$ for `Int64`) even when it overflows, because wrapping arithmetic *is* the ring arithmetic of that quotient, and the argument above uses only the semiring laws."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:152
msgid "Fraction-free determinant"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:154
msgid "Gaussian elimination over a field computes $\\det A$ as the product of pivots but divides at every step, which leaves the integers. Bareiss' algorithm keeps every intermediate value an integer.[^bareiss] Let $a^{(-1)}_{-1,-1} = 1$, $a^{(0)}_{ij} = a_{ij}$, and for $k = 0, 1, \\dots, n-2$ and $i, j > k$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:164
msgid "**Every value is a minor.** Let $M_k$ be the leading $k \\times k$ block of $A$ ($\\det M_0 = 1$), and for $i, j \\ge k$ let $d^{(k)}_{ij}$ be the *bordered minor* formed by rows $0, \\dots, k-1, i$ and columns $0, \\dots, k-1, j$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:179
msgid "We show $a^{(k)}_{ij} = d^{(k)}_{ij}$ (Sylvester's determinant identity). It is enough to prove the identity for matrices whose entries are independent indeterminates, over their field of fractions, where every $M_k$ is invertible; both sides are polynomials with integer coefficients, so it then holds in every commutative ring. Block elimination gives"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:190
msgid "where $S^{(k)} = (s^{(k)}_{ij})$ is the Schur complement of $M_k$ in $A$. The quotient property of Schur complements says that one more step of block elimination is one step of ordinary elimination on $S^{(k)}$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:200
msgid "Substituting,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:213
msgid "because $\\det M_k = d^{(k-1)}_{k-1,k-1}$. This is the recurrence, so by induction on $k$ the algorithm computes exactly the bordered minors. Two consequences follow. First, the division in the recurrence is *exact*: the quotient is a minor of $A$, so over an integral domain such as $\\mathbb{Z}$ the numerator is a multiple of the previous pivot and the quotient is unique. Second, the last value is the full determinant, $a^{(n-1)}_{n-1,n-1} = d^{(n-1)}_{n-1,n-1} = \\det A$. The attachment below gives the same proof in more detail."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:222
msgid "[Bareiss elimination: exactness and correctness](../../attachments/design_immut_bareiss.typ)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:224
msgid "**Pivoting.** The code chooses as pivot the candidate $a^{(k)}_{ik}$, $i \\ge k$, of largest `abs`. Row $i$ of $a^{(k)}$ depends only on row $i$ of $A$ and on rows $0, \\dots, k-1$, so exchanging two rows with indices $\\ge k$ before step $k$ is the same as running the algorithm on $A$ with those rows exchanged, which multiplies the final result by $-1$; the code tracks the sign. The previous pivot, the divisor, sits in row $k - 1$ and is not moved."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:231
msgid "**A zero pivot column.** If every candidate $a^{(k)}_{ik}$, $i \\ge k$, is zero, the code returns $0$ at once. This is correct: the previous pivot $\\det M_k$ is non-zero (otherwise the code would have stopped earlier), so $s^{(k)}_{ik} = d^{(k)}_{ik} / \\det M_k = 0$ for all $i \\ge k$. Column $k$ of the Schur complement $S^{(k)}$ is zero, so $\\det S^{(k)} = 0$ and"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:241
msgid "**Size of intermediate values.** Every intermediate value is a minor of $A$, so Hadamard's inequality bounds them:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:248
msgid "over the $k + 1$ rows involved (restricted to the chosen columns, which only decreases the norms). Let $H = \\prod_r \\max(1, \\lVert \\text{row}_r \\rVert_2)$ over all rows; every such product is at most $H$. The numerator of the recurrence is a difference of two products of two minors, so every product is at most $H^2$ and the numerator at most $2H^2$. This gives an overflow criterion for `Int`: if $2H^2 < 2^{31}$, no intermediate value overflows and the result is exact (for `Int64`, $2H^2 < 2^{63}$). Once a numerator wraps, the exact division no longer holds, so the result is wrong rather than correct modulo $2^{w}$. For `BigInt` the algorithm is always exact; it uses $O(n^3)$ arithmetic operations on integers of at most $\\log_2(2H^2) + 1$ bits."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:260
msgid "**Floating point.** The same bound explains why Bareiss elimination is a poor choice for `Double`. LU computes ratios of consecutive minors, of the size of the entries; Bareiss computes the minors themselves and multiplies two of them before dividing. For $A = 1000 I_{60}$ plus a superdiagonal of ones, $\\det A = 10^{180}$ is representable, but at step $k$ the numerator is about $10^{6(k+1)}$, which overflows after $k \\approx 50$; the method then returns NaN where LU returns $10^{180}$. Nor is there a tolerance: only an exactly zero pivot column is treated as singular."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:269
msgid "E. H. Bareiss, \"Sylvester's identity and multistep integer-preserving Gaussian elimination\", *Mathematics of Computation* 22 (1968), 565–578."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:272
msgid "**Small sizes.** For $n \\le 4$ the package uses closed formulas instead. For $n = 3$ it is the cofactor expansion along the first row; for $n = 4$ it is Laplace's expansion along the first two rows,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:281
msgid "six products of complementary $2 \\times 2$ minors. These formulas use only ring operations, so they are exact over every commutative ring and need no division at all. Over `Int` they are therefore exact modulo $2^{32}$ even when an intermediate value wraps, and the result is the true determinant whenever $|\\det A| < 2^{31}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:287
msgid "Lazy matrices"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:289
msgid "A `MatrixFn` is a pair $(\\text{shape}, f)$ with $f : [r] \\times [c] \\to T$. Operations compose functions: $\\mathtt{map}(g)$ is $g \\circ f$, transpose is $f \\circ \\mathrm{swap}$, and the product is"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:297
msgid "evaluated on demand. Nothing is cached, so reading one entry of a product costs the inner dimension $n$ times the cost of an entry of each factor. For a left-nested chain of $d$ products of $n \\times n$ matrices the cost $C_d$ of one entry satisfies $C_d = n(C_{d-1} + 1)$, so $C_d = \\Theta(n^d)$. Repeated squaring is worse, because both factors of $B \\cdot B$ are the same unevaluated $B$: $C_{t+1} = 2n\\, C_t$, so one entry of $A^{2^t}$ costs $(2n)^t$ reads of $A$, that is $k^{1 + \\log_2 n}$ for $k = 2^t$. Lazy powers are cheap to build and expensive to read."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:308
msgid "**Immutability.** No public operation modifies an existing `Matrix`, `Vector` or `MatrixFn`; `set` and `swap_*` return new values."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:310
msgid "**Shape invariants.** $r, c \\ge 0$ and the backing vector has exactly $rc$ elements; constructors reject anything else by aborting."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:312
msgid "**Bounds.** Every public read checks row and column separately, so a column index that would land inside the next row is rejected rather than read."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:314
msgid "**Exactness.** Over `BigInt`, `determinant` and `pow` are exact; over `Int` and `Int64`, `pow` is exact modulo $2^{w}$, and `determinant` is exact unless an intermediate product overflows ($n \\ge 5$) or the determinant itself does not fit ($n \\le 4$; see the Hadamard bound)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:318
msgid "**Empty cases.** $\\det$ and $\\operatorname{tr}$ of the $0 \\times 0$ matrix are $1$ and $0$; $A^0 = I$; a product with inner dimension $0$ is the zero matrix."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:321
msgid "**Complexity.** `set`: $O(\\log_{32} N)$; `map`, `+`, `transpose`, `swap_*`: $O(N)$; `matmul`: $rcn$ multiply-adds; `determinant`: $O(n^3)$; `pow`: at most $2\\lfloor\\log_2 k\\rfloor + 1$ products, $O(n^3 \\log k)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:327
msgid "**Floating-point LU for `immut` determinants.** It would need a field and a tolerance and would lose exactness for integer matrices, the main reason to use this package."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:330
msgid "**A runtime backend selector inside `Matrix`.** Backends are separate types ([`backends/default`](backends/default.md)); `Matrix` has one representation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:332
msgid "**Caching in `MatrixFn`.** It would make a pure value hold mutable state and change the cost model silently. Materialize with `Matrix::make` instead."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/immut.md:337
msgid "`immut` does not provide in-place updates, views, inverses, decompositions, eigenvalues, statistics or tolerance-based predicates; those belong to [`mutable`](mutable.md). It does not detect integer overflow, does not offer a checked `MatrixFn` API, and does not implement the [`algebra`](algebra.md) traits itself (the wrappers in `backends/default` do)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:1
msgid "internal design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:5
msgid "`immut` and `mutable` check the same preconditions (squareness, bounds, compatible shapes) and must report them identically. `internal` keeps one implementation of each check, so the two packages cannot drift apart, without exposing the helpers to downstream code."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:12
msgid "`immut` and `mutable` must report every precondition failure with the same kind and in the same order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:14
msgid "The helpers must not become public API, and the concrete packages must not depend on the experimental `algebra` layer for the shape method."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:19
msgid "An internal package"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:21
msgid "MoonBit's internal-package rule restricts imports of `Luna-Flow/linear-algebra/internal` to packages of this module. The helpers can therefore change freely, while their effects are specified on the public methods."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:26
msgid "`HasShape` separate from `MatrixShape`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:28
msgid "`@algebra.MatrixShape` has the same method, but `algebra` is experimental and the concrete packages should not depend on it. `HasShape` gives the guards a bound that `immut` and `mutable` implement locally. The repetition is the cost of keeping the stable concrete packages independent of the experimental layer."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:33
msgid "Row before column"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:35
msgid "`ensure_index_in_bounds` checks the row first, then the column, and each against its own dimension. Checking the flat offset $r c' + c$ against $rc'$ instead would accept a column index that spills into the next row."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:41
msgid "Each guard is the characteristic test of a domain from the [error design](error.md): $\\operatorname{dom}(\\operatorname{tr}) = \\{A : r = c\\}$, $\\operatorname{dom}(\\cdot) = \\{(A, B) : c_A = r_B\\}$, and so on. Writing the test once as a total function $\\chi : X \\to \\mathrm{Unit} + E$ gives both forms of a partial operation:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:53
msgid "Deriving the aborting guard from the checked one (`ensure_x` matches on `ensure_x_checked`) makes the two agree on every input by construction."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:58
msgid "`ensure_x(m)` aborts if and only if `ensure_x_checked(m)` returns `Err`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:59
msgid "Each checked guard returns the error kind listed on the [API page](../api/internal.md), with a fixed message."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:61
msgid "All guards run in $O(1)$ and only call `shape`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:65
msgid "**Duplicating the checks in each package**, which is how bounds behaviour diverged before the `0.4` line."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:67
msgid "**Public helpers.** They would become API that downstream code depends on."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/internal.md:71
msgid "`internal` checks shapes and indices only. It contains no arithmetic, no storage, and no checks that depend on values (singularity, emptiness of data, convergence)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:1
msgid "mutable design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:5
msgid "`mutable` is the execution-oriented half of the repository. It stores a matrix in one flat row-major array, lets callers update it in place and work through live views, and implements the floating-point numerical routines: determinant, inverse, rank, row reduction, Cholesky factorization, symmetric eigenvalues and the power method. The public API still reads like value operations wherever possible; mutation is confined to methods that return `Unit` and to the views, so callers can tell from a signature whether a call changes its receiver."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:16
msgid "The package must run on all four MoonBit targets (`native`, `js`, `wasm`, `wasm-gc`) with one public specification."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:18
msgid "It may depend only on `error`, `internal` and the upstream scalar packages, not on the experimental `algebra` and `container` layers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:20
msgid "Element types are generic. The numerical routines can only assume the `luna-generic` structure traits (`Field`, `Num`) plus `Compare`, `Sqrt` and this package's `Tolerance`; they cannot inspect the floating-point format."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:23
msgid "Its names, argument order and checked/unchecked conventions follow [`immut`](immut.md) wherever both packages offer an operation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:28
msgid "Flat row-major storage"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:30
msgid "**Options.** An array of row arrays, a persistent structure, or one flat array. **Decision.** One `Array[T]` with entry $(i, j)$ at $i c + j$, shared by all four targets. **Why.** It gives $O(1)$ access with one bounds check per coordinate, contiguous rows for the inner loops of elimination and products, and zero-cost row and column views. `from_array` adopts the caller's array so that large inputs need not be copied; the price is aliasing, which the API documents."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:38
msgid "Views instead of copies"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:40
msgid "`row_view`, `col_view` and `to_transpose` return live views in $O(1)$. A view is a pair (matrix, index) or a wrapper, so writes go to the shared storage and no synchronization is needed. Materializing is always explicit (`to_vector`, `materialize`, `transpose`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:45
msgid "Target-specific kernels with shared semantics"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:47
msgid "The package has one source file per target for the matrix, LU, view and transpose code. They differ only in loop structure (unrolling, packing, avoidance of division in index computations) chosen for each backend; the public semantics, including bounds and error behaviour, are identical, and the tests run on all four targets. Floating-point results may differ in the last bits because summation orders differ."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:54
msgid "Tolerance-based decisions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:56
msgid "Elimination must decide when a computed quantity \"is zero\". The package uses one absolute threshold $\\tau$ = `Tolerance::tolerance()` = $10^{-11}$ for `Double` and `Float`, applied as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:60
msgid "Routine"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:62
msgid "LU (`determinant` for $n \\ge 5$, `inverse`, `is_invertible`)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:62
msgid "pivot $\\lvert u_{kk}\\rvert < \\tau$ means singular"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:63
msgid "`inverse` of a diagonal matrix"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:63
msgid "$\\lvert a_{ii}\\rvert < \\tau$ means singular"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:64
msgid "largest remaining $\\lvert a_{ik}\\rvert < \\tau$ means no pivot; a multiplier $< \\tau$ is dropped"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:65
msgid "pivot candidate $\\le \\tau$ is set to zero and skipped; column entries $\\le \\tau$ are set to zero"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:66
msgid "radicand $\\le \\tau$ means not positive definite"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:67
msgid "`is_symmetric` (also the precondition of `eigen`)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:67
msgid "$\\lvert a_{ij} - a_{ji}\\rvert \\le \\tau$ counts as equal"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:68
msgid "residual $\\lVert Ax - \\lambda x\\rVert_\\infty \\le \\tau$ accepts; $\\lVert Ax\\rVert_\\infty \\le \\tau$ or $x^{\\mathsf T}x \\le \\tau$ gives `None`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:70
msgid "The structural fast paths and the internal decisions of `eigen` do not use $\\tau$: the fast paths require the structure exactly, and `eigen` deflates relative to the entries (see below). An absolute threshold is simple and predictable, but it is not scale invariant. Scaling $A$ by $10^{-12}$ makes every pivot fall below $\\tau$, so a perfectly conditioned matrix is reported singular; scaling by $10^{12}$ lets a numerically singular matrix pass. Scale data to order one before calling these routines. For `Float`, $\\tau = 10^{-11}$ is far below the unit roundoff $\\approx 6 \\times 10^{-8}$, so for data of order one the tests effectively check for exact zeros, and nearly singular `Float` matrices are not detected. The trait is closed (`pub`, not `pub(open)`), so these two instances are the only ones; a scale-aware tolerance policy is future work and would be a breaking change."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:83
msgid "Fast paths"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:85
msgid "`inverse` recognizes the identity (returns a copy), diagonal matrices (inverts the diagonal) and permutation matrices (returns the transpose, since $P^{\\mathsf T} P = I$ for a matrix whose columns are distinct coordinate vectors). `cholesky_decomposition` recognizes the identity and diagonal matrices, and `determinant` recognizes triangular matrices for $n \\ge 5$ and multiplies the diagonal. Each test costs $O(n^2)$ and saves an $O(n^3)$ factorization. The tests are exact (entries compared with $0$ and $1$), so a shortcut is taken only when it returns what the general algorithm would; a matrix that is merely close to the structure goes through the general path."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:95
msgid "Checked forms without changing the kernels"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:97
msgid "Every checked method validates (squareness, exponent sign, non-emptiness, lengths) and then calls its unchecked partner, which keeps the original aborting or `Option` behaviour. There is no checked matrix product here: `*` validates and aborts, and `unchecked_matmul` does not validate at all. This asymmetry with `@immut.Matrix::matmul` is known; a checked `matmul` would be an addition, not a change."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:104
msgid "Symmetric eigenvalues only"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:106
msgid "General real matrices can have complex eigenvalues, which a function returning `Vector[T]` cannot represent for real `T`. `eigen` therefore accepts only symmetric matrices, for which all eigenvalues are real and an orthonormal eigenbasis exists, and aborts otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:111
msgid "Which traits the routines require, and why"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:113
msgid "Bound"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:113
msgid "Needed for"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:115
msgid "products, `mul_vec`, `dot`, `trace`: sums of products starting from `0`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:116
msgid "`pow`: needs `1` for $A^0 = I$ and distributivity for associativity of the product"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:117
msgid "elimination divides by pivots; `mean` divides by $N$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:118
msgid "`abs` for pivot selection and every tolerance test"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:119
msgid "comparing magnitudes"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:120
msgid "Cholesky diagonal, Householder norms, Givens rotations, $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_F$, `std_dev`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:121
msgid "the zero decisions above"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:123
msgid "`Field` is commutative in `luna-generic`, which the derivations below use (for example $\\det(LU) = \\det L \\det U$ and $(AB)^{\\mathsf T} = B^{\\mathsf T} A^{\\mathsf T}$)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:128
msgid "Throughout, $u$ is the unit roundoff ($2^{-53}$ for `Double`, $2^{-24}$ for `Float`), $\\mathrm{fl}(x \\circ y) = (x \\circ y)(1 + \\delta)$ with $|\\delta| \\le u$, and"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:138
msgid "The second fact[^gamma] is what turns \"each operation is rounded\" into bounds for whole algorithms. Inequalities between matrices hold entry-wise, and $|A|$ is the matrix of absolute values. A flop is one floating-point addition, subtraction, multiplication or division."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:143
msgid "Higham, Lemma 3.1. The proof is an induction on $n$ using $|\\theta_{n+1}| \\le |\\theta_n| + u + u|\\theta_n|$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:146
msgid "Matrix product"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:148
msgid "$(AB)_{ik} = \\sum_j a_{ij} b_{jk}$ costs $rcn$ multiply-adds, $2rcn$ flops. The kernels sum in different orders (four partial products per step in the unrolled kernels; a packed copy of the columns of $B$ for products of at least $4 \\times 16 \\times 16$), but in any order each product $a_{ij} b_{jk}$ passes through one multiplication and at most $n - 1$ additions, so it carries a factor $1 + \\theta_n$ and"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:159
msgid "Results on different targets therefore agree to that accuracy, not bit for bit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:162
msgid "LU factorization with partial pivoting"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:164
msgid "For square $A$, Gaussian elimination with partial pivoting computes a permutation $P$, a unit lower triangular $L$ and an upper triangular $U$ with"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:171
msgid "**Derivation.** Write $A^{(0)} = A$. At step $k$ the algorithm chooses the row $p \\ge k$ with the largest $|a^{(k)}_{pk}|$ and swaps it into position $k$ (a transposition $P_k$). It then subtracts multiples of row $k$ from the rows below, which is multiplication by the Gauss transform"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:182
msgid "so that $a^{(k+1)}_{ij} = a^{(k)}_{ij} - l_{ik} a^{(k)}_{kj}$ and column $k$ below the diagonal becomes zero. After $n - 1$ steps"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:189
msgid "Since $e_k^{\\mathsf T} \\ell_k = 0$, $M_k^{-1} = I + \\ell_k e_k^{\\mathsf T}$, and a later transposition $P_j$ ($j > k$) only exchanges entries of $\\ell_k$: $P_j M_k = \\tilde M_k P_j$ with $\\tilde M_k = I - (P_j \\ell_k) e_k^{\\mathsf T}$. Moving all permutations to the right gives $U = \\tilde M_{n-2} \\cdots \\tilde M_0 P A$ with $P = P_{n-2} \\cdots P_0$, hence $PA = LU$ with"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:200
msgid "This is why the code can store the multipliers in place of the eliminated entries and swap whole rows, multipliers included: the stored lower triangle is exactly $L$. The pivot choice gives $|l_{ik}| \\le 1$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:204
msgid "**Cost.** Step $k$ updates an $(n-k-1) \\times (n-k-1)$ block with one multiply and one subtract per entry, so"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:211
msgid "flops."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:213
msgid "**Determinant.** Taking determinants of $PA = LU$ with $\\det L = 1$ and $\\det P = (-1)^{s}$ for $s$ exchanges,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:220
msgid "**Solving and the inverse.** $Ax = b$ becomes $L y = P b$ (forward substitution, $y_i = (Pb)_i - \\sum_{k<i} l_{ik} y_k$) and $U x = y$ (back substitution, $x_i = (y_i - \\sum_{k>i} u_{ik} x_k) / u_{ii}$). Each costs $\\sum_i 2i \\approx n^2$ flops per right-hand side. The inverse is the solution for the $n$ columns of $I$; the code does not exploit the zeros of $e_j$, so the total is $\\tfrac23 n^3 + n \\cdot 2n^2 = \\tfrac83 n^3$ flops."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:227
msgid "**Stability.** The computed factors satisfy the backward error bound (Wilkinson; Higham, Theorem 9.3)[^higham]"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:234
msgid "To turn this into a norm bound, let $\\rho_n = \\max_{i,j,k} |a^{(k)}_{ij}| / \\max_{i,j} |a_{ij}|$ be the growth factor. Every row of $\\hat L$ has at most $n$ entries of magnitude at most $1$, so $\\lVert \\hat L \\rVert_\\infty \\le n$, and every row of $\\hat U$ has at most $n$ entries bounded by $\\rho_n \\max |a_{ij}| \\le \\rho_n \\lVert A \\rVert_\\infty$, so"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:247
msgid "Partial pivoting bounds the growth: since $|l_{ik}| \\le 1$, $|a^{(k+1)}_{ij}| \\le |a^{(k)}_{ij}| + |a^{(k)}_{kj}| \\le 2 \\max |a^{(k)}|$, so $\\rho_n \\le 2^{n-1}$. The bound is attained only by contrived matrices; in practice $\\rho_n$ is small and the method is backward stable. Including the two triangular solves, each computed solution satisfies $(A + \\Delta A)\\hat x = b$ with $\\lVert \\Delta A \\rVert_\\infty \\le 2 n^2 \\gamma_n \\rho_n \\lVert A \\rVert_\\infty$ (Higham, Theorem 9.5), and the *forward* error is governed by the condition number $\\kappa_\\infty(A) = \\lVert A \\rVert_\\infty \\lVert A^{-1} \\rVert_\\infty$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:262
msgid "Each column of the computed inverse obeys this bound."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:264
msgid "N. J. Higham, *Accuracy and Stability of Numerical Algorithms*, 2nd ed., SIAM, 2002, chapters 3 (basics), 8 (triangular systems), 9 (LU) and 10 (Cholesky)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:268
msgid "Closed forms for small determinants"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:270
msgid "For $n \\le 4$ the determinant is evaluated by formulas instead of LU: the rule $ad - bc$, cofactor expansion along the first row for $n = 3$, and for $n = 4$ Laplace's expansion along the first two rows into six products of complementary $2 \\times 2$ minors,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:279
msgid "where $\\overline{\\{p,q\\}}$ is the complementary pair of columns. The sign is $(-1)^{1+p+q}$: $+$ for $\\{0,1\\}, \\{0,3\\}, \\{1,2\\}, \\{2,3\\}$ and $-$ for $\\{0,2\\}, \\{1,3\\}$, which is the pattern $s_{01} c_{23} - s_{02} c_{13} + s_{03} c_{12} + s_{12} c_{03} - s_{13} c_{02} + s_{23} c_{01}$ in the code."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:285
msgid "They use no division and no pivoting decision, which avoids the tolerance test for tiny matrices. Their rounding error is easy to bound: every one of the $n!$ monomials $\\pm a_{0\\sigma(0)} \\cdots a_{n-1,\\sigma(n-1)}$ passes through at most $m$ roundings, with $m = 2, 5, 10$ for $n = 2, 3, 4$ (for $n = 4$: two in each $2 \\times 2$ minor, one for their product, five for the sum of six terms), so"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:296
msgid "The permanent is at least $|\\det A|$, and much larger when the monomials cancel; then the relative accuracy is lost, exactly as the determinant itself is ill-conditioned there. These formulas are not backward stable in the LU sense."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:301
msgid "Rank and reduced row echelon form"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:303
msgid "`rank` runs elimination with partial pivoting on a copy and counts pivots whose magnitude is at least the tolerance, moving to the next column when the largest remaining entry is below it. This is the *numerical rank* with an absolute threshold $\\tau$. It is exact for matrices whose pivots are well separated from $\\tau$ and otherwise depends on scaling. The singular value decomposition gives the reliable numerical rank, $\\#\\{\\sigma_i > \\tau\\}$, because by the Eckart–Young theorem $\\sigma_{k+1}$ is the 2-norm distance to the nearest matrix of rank $k$; elimination pivots have no such meaning, and a matrix with all pivots of order one can be within $2^{-n}$ of a singular one.[^kahan] The SVD is not implemented."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:314
msgid "Kahan's example: the upper triangular matrix with $1$ on the diagonal and $-1$ above it has all pivots equal to $1$, while its smallest singular value decreases like $2^{-n}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:318
msgid "`reduce_row_elimination` is Gauss–Jordan elimination: each pivot row is scaled to make the pivot one, and the pivot column is cleared in every other row. For each of at most $\\min(r, c)$ pivots it updates all $r$ rows over all $c$ columns, about $2 r c \\min(r, c)$ flops, and works in place. Unlike LU it produces the reduced form, in which every pivot column is a coordinate vector, so the non-pivot columns of the result express the dependent columns of $A$ in terms of the pivot columns."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:326
msgid "Cholesky factorization"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:328
msgid "A symmetric positive definite (SPD) matrix has a unique factorization $A = L L^{\\mathsf T}$ with $L$ lower triangular and $l_{jj} > 0$. Comparing entries of $A = L L^{\\mathsf T}$ for $i \\ge j$,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:339
msgid "Each equation determines one new entry from earlier ones, which proves uniqueness once the radicands are positive. The code evaluates them row by row (the Cholesky–Banachiewicz order) and reads only the lower triangle of $A$; the cost is $\\sum_j 2 j (n - j) \\approx \\tfrac13 n^3$ flops, half of LU, because symmetry halves the work."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:345
msgid "**Why success characterizes SPD.** Write $D = \\operatorname{diag}(l_{jj})$. Then $A = (L D^{-1})(D L^{\\mathsf T})$ is an LU factorization without pivoting, whose pivots are $u_{jj} = l_{jj}^2$, the radicands. The product of the first $j + 1$ pivots is the leading principal minor $\\det M_{j+1}$, so the radicand at step $j$ equals $\\det M_{j+1} / \\det M_j$. By Sylvester's criterion $A$ is SPD exactly when all leading principal minors are positive, so in exact arithmetic the factorization succeeds exactly for SPD matrices. With the tolerance, `cholesky_decomposition` succeeds exactly when every ratio $\\det M_{j+1} / \\det M_j$ exceeds $\\tau$ (up to rounding), and `is_positive_definite` is implemented by attempting it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:356
msgid "**Stability.** Cholesky needs no pivoting: from $a_{jj} = \\sum_k l_{jk}^2$ every $|l_{jk}| \\le \\sqrt{a_{jj}}$, so entries cannot grow, and the computed factor satisfies $\\hat L \\hat L^{\\mathsf T} = A + \\Delta A$ with $|\\Delta A| \\le \\gamma_{n+1} |\\hat L|\\,|\\hat L^{\\mathsf T}|$ (Higham, Theorem 10.3). Since $(|\\hat L|\\,|\\hat L^{\\mathsf T}|)_{ij} \\le \\sqrt{a_{ii} a_{jj}}$ up to $O(u)$ by the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality, the backward error is small relative to the diagonal of $A$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:365
msgid "Symmetric eigenvalue problem"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:367
msgid ""
"For real symmetric $A$ the spectral theorem gives $A = Q \\Lambda Q^{\\mathsf T}$ with $Q$ orthogonal and $\\Lambda$ real diagonal. `eigen` computes it in two phases for $n \\n"
"e 2$, and by a closed formula for $n = 2$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:371
msgid ""
"**Householder tridiagonalization.** For $x \\in \\mathbb{R}^m$, $x \\n"
"e 0$, let $\\alpha = -\\operatorname{sgn}(x_{m-1}) \\lVert x \\rVert_2$ and $v = x - \\alpha e_{m-1}$. The reflector $H = I - 2 v v^{\\mathsf T} / v^{\\mathsf T} v$ is symmetric and orthogonal, and it maps $x$ to $\\alpha e_{m-1}$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:384
msgid "The sign of $\\alpha$ is opposite to $x_{m-1}$, so $x_{m-1} - \\alpha$ adds two numbers of the same sign and cannot cancel. The code (the `tred2` scheme) works from the last row upwards: for row $i$ it reflects $x = (a_{i0}, \\dots, a_{i,i-1})$ onto its last coordinate, applies $H$ from both sides, and so zeroes row and column $i$ outside the tridiagonal band. After $n - 2$ such steps"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:395
msgid "where $T$ is symmetric tridiagonal with diagonal $d$ and off-diagonal $e$. The two-sided update costs $\\tfrac43 n^3$ flops and accumulating $Q_1$ explicitly another $\\tfrac43 n^3$. Before forming each reflector the row is divided by $\\sum_k |x_k|$, which avoids overflow and underflow in $\\lVert x \\rVert_2$; only an exactly zero row is left unreflected."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:401
msgid "**Implicit QL with Wilkinson shifts.** The tridiagonal $T$ is diagonalized by plane rotations. Before each sweep on the unreduced block starting at $l$, the shift $\\sigma$ is the eigenvalue of the leading $2 \\times 2$ block $\\begin{pmatrix} d_l & e_l \\\\ e_l & d_{l+1} \\end{pmatrix}$ closer to $d_l$. With $g = (d_{l+1} - d_l)/(2 e_l)$, its eigenvalues are"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:412
msgid "because $\\tfrac12(d_l + d_{l+1}) = d_l + e_l g$ and the square root equals $|e_l| \\sqrt{g^2 + 1}$ (the sign of $e_l$ is absorbed by $\\pm$). The one closer to $d_l$ makes $|g \\pm \\sqrt{g^2+1}|$ small, that is, takes the sign opposite to $g$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:422
msgid "where the second form follows from $(g - s\\sqrt{g^2+1})(g + s\\sqrt{g^2+1}) = g^2 - (g^2 + 1) = -1$ for $s = \\operatorname{sgn}(g)$, and avoids the cancellation of the first. The code uses it with $\\operatorname{sgn}(0) = 1$. A sweep starts from $d_m - \\sigma$ and applies Givens rotations $\\begin{pmatrix} c & s \\\\ -s & c \\end{pmatrix}$, $c^2 + s^2 = 1$, from the bottom of the block upwards, each one chasing the bulge created by the previous; $Q$ is updated with the same rotations, $6n$ flops each. The rotation parameters are computed as $c = g/f$, $r = \\sqrt{c^2 + 1}$, $s = 1/r$, $c = c s$ (or the symmetric form), dividing by the larger of $|f|, |g|$ so that no square of a large number is formed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:434
msgid "An off-diagonal entry is treated as zero when it is negligible next to its diagonal neighbours in the working precision,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:441
msgid "that is, $|e_m| \\lesssim u\\,(|d_m| + |d_{m+1}|)$, and the block then splits. The test needs no knowledge of $u$, so it works for `Float` and `Double` alike, and it is invariant under scaling of $A$. For symmetric tridiagonal matrices the Wilkinson shift converges for every input in exact arithmetic (Wilkinson, 1968), and typically cubically; about two sweeps per eigenvalue is usual. The code nevertheless aborts after 60 sweeps for one starting index $l$. With accumulation of $Q$ the whole computation takes about $9 n^3$ flops (Golub and Van Loan's estimate for the symmetric QR algorithm), $\\tfrac83 n^3$ of them in the reduction."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:451
msgid "**Accuracy.** Every transformation is orthogonal, so in exact arithmetic the spectrum never changes, and rounding contributes a backward error of $O(n u) \\lVert A \\rVert_2$; setting a negligible $e_m$ to zero perturbs $T$ by at most $u(|d_m| + |d_{m+1}|) \\le 2u \\lVert A \\rVert_2$, which is of the same order. Weyl's inequality, $|\\lambda_i(A + E) - \\lambda_i(A)| \\le \\lVert E \\rVert_2$ for symmetric $A$ and $E$, then gives for the computed eigenvalues"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:462
msgid "with a modest $c_n$, independent of the scale of $A$. Eigenvectors are accurate in proportion to $u \\lVert A \\rVert_2/\\text{gap}$, where the gap is the distance to the nearest other eigenvalue (the Davis–Kahan theorem). The only absolute test left is the symmetry check $|a_{ij} - a_{ji}| \\le \\tau$ before the computation; the algorithm then reads the lower triangle."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:468
msgid "**The $2 \\times 2$ case.** The code works with the symmetric part $\\begin{pmatrix} a & s \\\\ s & d \\end{pmatrix}$, $s = \\tfrac12(a_{01} + a_{10})$. Its characteristic polynomial $\\lambda^2 - (a + d)\\lambda + (ad - s^2)$ has the discriminant"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:477
msgid "a sum of squares, so with $m = \\tfrac12(a + d)$ and $h = \\tfrac12(a - d)$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:483
msgid "Computing $r^2$ as $m^2 - \\det A$ instead would subtract two numbers of size $m^2$ and lose about $u\\,m^2$ in absolute terms, which for $m \\approx 10^8$ is about $1$: the computed discriminant could even be negative for a diagonal matrix. The code evaluates $r$ as $\\beta\\sqrt{(h/\\beta)^2 + (s/\\beta)^2}$ with $\\beta = \\max(|h|, |s|)$, which cannot overflow, and never thresholds it, so eigenvalues as close as rounding allows stay distinct. The eigenvalue of larger magnitude, $m + \\operatorname{sgn}(m)\\, r$, is a sum of two numbers of the same sign and is computed directly; the other follows from $\\lambda_1 \\lambda_2 = \\det A = ad - s^2$, which avoids the cancellation in $m - r$ when $|\\lambda_2| \\ll |\\lambda_1|$. When $s = 0$ the matrix is diagonal and the code returns $a$ and $d$ exactly, in decreasing order, with the coordinate vectors."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:496
msgid "For the eigenvector of $\\lambda_1$, both"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:504
msgid "solve $(A - \\lambda_1 I)v = 0$: for the first, $(a - \\lambda_1)(h + r) + s^2 = (h - r)(h + r) + s^2 = h^2 - r^2 + s^2 = 0$, and the second row is $s(h + r) + (d - \\lambda_1)s = s(h + r - h - r) = 0$; the second vector is checked the same way. The code takes the first when $h \\ge 0$ and the second when $h < 0$, so that $h + r$ or $r - h$ adds numbers of the same sign and cannot cancel. Since $A$ is symmetric, the eigenvector of $\\lambda_2$ is orthogonal to it, $(v_y, -v_x)$. Each column is scaled so that its entry of largest magnitude is $1$; the columns are therefore never zero, never parallel, and paired with the right eigenvalues."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:514
msgid "Power method"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:516
msgid "The code starts from $x_0 = (1, \\dots, 1)$, or, if $\\lVert A x_0 \\rVert_\\infty \\le \\tau$, from the first coordinate vector $e_k$ with $\\lVert A e_k \\rVert_\\infty > \\tau$; if there is none it returns `None`. It then iterates"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:526
msgid "and stops when $\\lVert A x_k - \\lambda_k x_k \\rVert_\\infty \\le \\tau$; the normalization makes $\\lVert x_k \\rVert_\\infty = 1$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:529
msgid ""
"**Convergence.** If $A$ is diagonalizable with eigenvalues $|\\lambda_1| > |\\lambda_2| \\ge \\dots$ and eigenvectors $v_i$, and $x_0$ has a component $c_1 \\n"
"e 0$ along $v_1$, then"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:537
msgid "so the direction of $x_k$ converges to $v_1$ linearly with ratio $r = |\\lambda_2 / \\lambda_1|$. For negative $\\lambda_1$ the sign of $x_k$ alternates, which the Rayleigh quotient does not notice. For symmetric $A$ with orthonormal $v_i$, $x_k \\propto \\sum_i c_i (\\lambda_i/\\lambda_1)^k v_i$ gives"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:548
msgid ""
"so the eigenvalue converges twice as fast as the vector. When $|\\lambda_1| = |\\lambda_2|$ with $\\lambda_1 \\n"
"e \\lambda_2$ (for example $\\pm 1$) the direction oscillates and the residual test never passes, and when $A$ is nilpotent the iterate reaches zero; both cases return `None`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:553
msgid "**What the stopping test guarantees.** For symmetric $A$, a residual $r = Ax - \\lambda x$ bounds the distance to the spectrum: expanding $x$ in the orthonormal eigenbasis, $\\lVert r \\rVert_2^2 = \\sum_i c_i^2 (\\lambda_i - \\lambda)^2 \\ge \\min_i (\\lambda_i - \\lambda)^2 \\lVert x \\rVert_2^2$. With $\\lVert r \\rVert_\\infty \\le \\tau$ and $\\lVert x \\rVert_\\infty = 1$ we have $\\lVert r \\rVert_2 \\le \\sqrt n\\, \\tau$ and $\\lVert x \\rVert_2 \\ge 1$, so some eigenvalue lies within $\\sqrt n\\, \\tau$ of the returned $\\lambda$. The test is absolute, so for $\\lVert A \\rVert \\gg 1$ it may be unreachable in floating point (the residual cannot drop below about $u \\lVert A \\rVert$), and for $\\lVert A \\rVert \\ll 1$ it passes before $x$ is accurate."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:564
msgid "Statistics"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:566
msgid "`variance` is the population variance computed in two passes: first the mean $\\bar a$, then $\\tfrac1N \\sum (a_i - \\bar a)^2$. The one-pass formula $\\tfrac1N \\sum a_i^2 - \\bar a^2$ subtracts two nearly equal numbers when the data have a large mean and a small spread, and can even return a negative value. With $S = \\sum (a_i - \\bar a)^2$ and the condition number $\\kappa = \\lVert a \\rVert_2 / \\sqrt S$ of the data, Chan, Golub and LeVeque (1983) show that the relative error of $S$ is bounded by about $N \\kappa^2 u$ for the one-pass formula and by about $N u + N^2 \\kappa^2 u^2$ for the two-pass one, which is $O(Nu)$ unless $\\kappa$ is near $u^{-1/2}$. The count $N$ is accumulated as a sum of ones in `T`, exact up to $2^{53}$ entries for `Double` and $2^{24}$ for `Float`; beyond that the count stops growing and the mean is wrong."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:579
msgid "Transpose views and products"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:581
msgid "`Transpose::mul` computes $A^{\\mathsf T} B^{\\mathsf T}$ as $(BA)^{\\mathsf T}$ by reusing the matrix kernel on the wrapped matrices. Entry by entry,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:589
msgid "which agree when the scalars commute. All scalar types with `Tolerance` (`Float`, `Double`) commute, but `Transpose::mul` only requires `AddMonoid + Mul`; for a non-commutative scalar type the result is the product in the wrong order (see the [algebra design](algebra.md))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:596
msgid "**Storage.** `data.length() == row * col` at all times; every public accessor checks the row and column separately."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:598
msgid "**Value-returning methods do not mutate.** Only `Unit`-returning methods, view writes and `reduce_row_elimination` change a matrix."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:600
msgid "**Checked/unchecked law.** `x.f() == Ok(x.unchecked_f())` whenever the precondition holds; `inverse` returns `Err(SingularMatrix)` exactly when `unchecked_inverse` returns `None`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:603
msgid "**Determinant consistency.** For $n \\ge 5$ and a matrix that is not triangular, `determinant` returns exactly `0` when the LU factorization reports a pivot below $\\tau$, which is also when `is_invertible` returns `false` and `inverse` fails; otherwise it returns a product of pivots that are at least $\\tau$ in magnitude, which can still underflow. For $n \\le 4$ the closed formulas are used for `determinant`, while `is_invertible` still uses LU, so a matrix with a tiny non-zero determinant can be \"not invertible\" with a non-zero `determinant`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:611
msgid "**Residual guarantees.** `cholesky_decomposition` returns a factor with backward error $O(nu)$ relative to $|\\hat L|\\,|\\hat L^{\\mathsf T}|$; `eigen` returns eigenpairs with backward error $O(nu)\\lVert A \\rVert$, for every scale of $A$; `power_method` returns only pairs with residual at most $\\tau$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:616
msgid "**Complexity.** `*`: $2rcn$ flops; `determinant`: $\\tfrac23 n^3$; `inverse`: $\\tfrac83 n^3$; `rank`, `reduce_row_elimination`: $O(rc\\min(r, c))$; `cholesky_decomposition`: $\\tfrac13 n^3$; `eigen`: about $9n^3$; `power_method`: two matrix-vector products, $4n^2$ flops, per iteration; statistics: $O(rc)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:624
msgid "**A relative or norm-scaled tolerance.** More robust, but it would change the results of existing callers; deferred until a tolerance policy can be passed explicitly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:627
msgid "**Returning complex eigenvalues.** Would make this package depend on a complex number type and change the signature for real symmetric input."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:629
msgid "**Copy-on-write views.** Would hide the cost of writes and break the in-place contract that views exist for."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:631
msgid "**A single portable kernel.** Measured to be slower on some targets; the per-target files trade code size for speed while sharing one specification."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/mutable.md:636
msgid "`mutable` does not provide linear solves for a right-hand side as a public method (only the inverse), QR, SVD or least-squares solvers, eigenvalues of non-symmetric matrices, sparse storage, condition number estimates, or scale-aware tolerances. It does not implement the [`algebra`](algebra.md) traits itself; [`backends/default`](backends/default.md) wraps it for that. Domain workflows such as regression or optimization belong in downstream packages."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:1
msgid "perf design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:5
msgid "`perf` lets the standard `moon bench` tool measure the steady-state cost of the `@mutable` numerical kernels on every registered case, with no extra infrastructure, so that a kernel change can be compared before and after."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:11
msgid "Measurements must use the standard `moon bench` tool and run on `native`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:12
msgid "Fixture loading and input preparation must stay outside the timed region."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:16
msgid "Preparation outside the timed region"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:18
msgid "Inputs are prepared once per case before registering the benchmark. Only the kernel call and the checksum fold are inside the timed closure."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:21
msgid "Keep the checksum"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:23
msgid "Passing the checksum to `b.keep` makes the result observable, so the compiler cannot eliminate the call, while costing only one 64-bit fold per output value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:26
msgid "Not part of the default gate"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:28
msgid "Benchmarks depend on the machine and take long; they are excluded from `run_test.sh` unless `LINEAR_ALGEBRA_TEST_BENCH=1` is set."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:33
msgid "`moon bench` repeats each benchmark body many times and reports statistics of the per-iteration time. A steady-state measurement estimates the expected cost $\\mathbb{E}[T]$ of one call after warm-up, excluding process start, fixture I/O and first-call effects. The repository's report scripts summarize samples with robust statistics: the median, the nearest-rank 90th percentile, and the median absolute deviation $\\operatorname{MAD} = \\operatorname{median}_i |t_i - \\operatorname{median}(t)|$. Unlike the mean and the standard deviation, the median and the MAD have a breakdown point of 50%: a few samples disturbed by the operating system cannot move them arbitrarily."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:45
msgid "Every registered case is benchmarked exactly once per run, under a name that identifies operation and case."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:50
msgid "**A hand-written timing loop.** `moon bench` already handles iteration counts and reporting; [`perf_runner`](perf_runner.md) covers the cases where raw samples are needed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf.md:56
msgid "`perf` does not compute or store statistics, does not compare against baselines, and does not include cold-start timing."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:1
msgid "perf_runner design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:5
msgid "`moon bench` reports summaries; the repository's report pipeline needs raw per-sample timings of a single case, under its own control of repetition and warm-up, and a way to replay a case and check its result. `perf_runner` is that single-case tool."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:12
msgid "The report pipeline needs raw samples of one case per process, with control over repetition and warm-up."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:14
msgid "The executable may not depend on a serialization library."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:18
msgid "Scratch copies outside the timer"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:20
msgid "For cases that mutate their inputs, `repeat` copies are prepared *before* `monotonic_clock_start`, so copying is not measured and every call sees the same input."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:24
msgid "Checksums in every mode"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:26
msgid "Each call's checksum is folded into a running value, so the work stays observable and the diagnostic mode can verify bit-identical results."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:29
msgid "Plain JSON lines"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:31
msgid "The output is one JSON object per run, assembled by string concatenation, which keeps the runner free of serialization dependencies and easy to parse from Python."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:37
msgid "A sample is the mean time of `repeat` consecutive calls, $t = \\tfrac1{\\text{repeat}} \\sum_{j=1}^{\\text{repeat}} T_j$. Averaging inside a sample reduces timer resolution error, which is fixed per measurement, by the factor `repeat`, and reduces independent per-call noise by $1/\\sqrt{\\text{repeat}}$. Across samples the pipeline uses order statistics (median, p90) and the MAD, which are robust to outliers caused by scheduling. Warm-up rounds discard the transient of the first calls (cache, branch predictors, lazy initialization)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:48
msgid "In diagnostic mode, the printed checksum equals the FNV-style fold of the per-call checksums, which the package's whitebox tests verify."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:50
msgid "Copies made for scratch cases never alias the prepared inputs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:54
msgid "**Timing each call separately.** Sub-microsecond calls are below the clock resolution; `repeat` amortizes it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_runner.md:59
msgid "The runner measures one case per process, computes no statistics itself and supports only cases registered in `perf_support`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:1
msgid "perf_support design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:5
msgid "Benchmarks of numerical kernels are only useful if they are reproducible and comparable: the same inputs on every run and every machine, measurements of the computation rather than of setup, and a guarantee that the measured code actually ran. `perf_support` provides those guarantees for the benchmark subsystem of this repository."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:13
msgid "Inputs must be identical on every run and machine, without storing large arrays in the repository's source code."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:15
msgid "The measured work must stay observable, so that the compiler cannot remove it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:17
msgid "The package serves only this repository's benchmark tools and the `native` target."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:22
msgid "Fixtures at runtime, registry in code"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:24
msgid "The case list is generated into MoonBit code (`generated_registry.mbt`) from `bench/datasets/manifest.json`, while the large input arrays live in JSON files that are loaded at runtime. This keeps the benchmark binary small, so that code size does not distort the measurements, and lets the Rust baseline consume the same fixtures."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:30
msgid "Self-healing fixtures"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:32
msgid "When a fixture file is missing, it is regenerated from the registry seed and written out, so a clean checkout can run any case. When a file exists but its version, metadata or shape disagrees with the registry, the run aborts: a silently mismatched input would make the numbers meaningless."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:37
msgid "Mutation policy"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:39
msgid "Some operations change their input (for example `reduce_row_elimination`). Cases with `mutation_policy = \"scratch_per_sample\"` therefore run on fresh copies, so every sample measures the same work."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:43
msgid "Unchecked kernels"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:45
msgid "The benchmarks call the `unchecked_*` methods, because the inputs are known to satisfy the preconditions and the cost of validation is not what is being measured."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:51
msgid "Deterministic inputs"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:53
msgid ""
"Inputs are generated from a per-case seed with the SplitMix64 generator and mapped to a target distribution (for example uniform on $[-0.9, 0.9]$, from the top 53 bits of each 64-bit draw). Structured families are built from such draws by construction, for example a symmetric positive definite matrix as $M^{\\mathsf T} M + \\alpha I$, which is SPD for any $\\alpha > 0$ because $x^{\\mathsf T}(M^{\\mathsf T}M + \\alpha I)x = \\lVert Mx \\rVert^2 + \\alpha\\lVert x\\rVert^2 > 0$ for $x \\n"
"e 0$. The same seed always gives the same bits, so a fixture can be regenerated instead of stored."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:62
msgid "Checksums"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:64
msgid "Each run folds the bit patterns of its result into a 64-bit value with an FNV-style mix,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:72
msgid "where $w_k$ is the IEEE bit pattern of the $k$-th output value (shape first for matrices). The checksum serves two purposes: it keeps the compiler from removing the computation as dead code, and it detects a change of result bits between runs, targets or versions. It is not a cryptographic hash."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:79
msgid "For a fixed dataset version, `prepare_case(c)` yields bit-identical inputs on every run."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:81
msgid "`run_prepared_case_inplace(p, true)` leaves `p` unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:82
msgid "The checksum depends on every output value and on the output shape."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:86
msgid "**Embedding all inputs in code.** Large literal arrays inflate the binary and compile time."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:88
msgid "**Random inputs per run.** Results would not be comparable across runs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/perf_support.md:92
msgid "`perf_support` measures nothing itself; timing and statistics are in [`perf_runner`](perf_runner.md), [`perf`](perf.md) and `bench/run.py`. It covers only the `@mutable` package with `Double` inputs, and it is not part of the default test gate."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:1
msgid "linear-algebra"
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:3
msgid "This manual documents release `0.5.0` of `Luna-Flow/linear-algebra`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:7
msgid "`Luna-Flow/linear-algebra` is the linear-algebra layer of Luna Flow. It provides dense matrices and vectors in two execution models, value-oriented (`immut`) and in-place (`mutable`), numerical routines for floating-point matrices, exact algorithms for integer matrices, and small capability layers (`algebra`, `container`) that let generic algorithms run on any matrix or vector type that implements them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:14
msgid "Release `0.5.0` centers on three changes:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:16
msgid "The module builds with the MoonBit 0.10 toolchain and its `moon.mod` / `moon.pkg` manifests."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:18
msgid "Indexing is provided by ordinary methods (`at`, `set`, `get`) with `#alias(\"_[_]\")`; only the trait methods listed in each package's `extends.mbt` are callable with method syntax."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:21
msgid "The native OpenBLAS backend is withdrawn from the module, because its upstream binding does not compile with MoonBit 0.10 (see [Outside the module](#outside-the-module))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:25
msgid "Install"
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:31
msgid "Then import the packages you use in your `moon.pkg`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:42
msgid "Generic code over scalar types also needs the upstream packages `Luna-Flow/luna-generic` (`0.4.0`) and `Luna-Flow/arithmetic` (`0.5.0`). The module needs the MoonBit toolchain 0.10 or later (`moonc` ≥ 0.10) and builds without warnings with `moon check --target all`. All packages support the `wasm-gc`, `js`, `wasm` and `native` targets; `mutable` has a tuned kernel file per target, and the benchmark packages run on `native`."
msgstr ""

